;; The module _ssl (queue 209, PYS22 (a); CPython 3.14.10's Modules/_ssl.c and its clinic file) over Go's
;; crypto/tls: ssl.py runs unchanged on it. A context (_SSLContext) keeps OpenSSL's settings as _ssl.c reads and
;; writes them (options, verify mode and flags, host flags, the protocol bounds, the cipher list, ALPN, the
;; certificate chain and key, the trust store) and makes a tls.Config of them for each connection. An SSL object
;; (_SSLSocket, over a socket or a pair of MemoryBIOs) drives a tls.Conn in a goroutine of its own over an
;; in-memory transport (builtins_ssl_conn.go), so OpenSSL's non-blocking contract (SSL_ERROR_WANT_READ, a
;; handshake resumed by calling again) holds for both. Verification is _ssl's own over Go's x509 (the codes and
;; messages of OpenSSL's X509_verify_cert_error_string), certificates decoded as _decode_certificate does
;; (builtins_ssl_cert.go). What Go's TLS does not have is by design (doc/python/PYTHON-SURVEY.md, "As built: 209").


